  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on an unweighted mean of indicators to construct its summary index, which assumes each of the 23 indicators contributes equally to physical activity promotion.",
      "what": "The study evaluated 23 indicators of physical activity infrastructure across 34 municipalities and 1,623 neighborhoods in Greater Santiago, Chile, grouping them into four dimensions: active transport, green areas, public transport, and safety. Green areas were identified as the most unequally distributed resource, while public transport was the most evenly distributed, even favoring lower-income municipalities. Except for public transport, the variation in infrastructure quality was greater within individual municipalities than between different ones.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that urban infrastructure disparities are more pronounced at the neighborhood scale than at the municipal scale, proving that municipal-level analyses mask significant localized inequalities and that urban interventions must target smaller geographic units."
    }
  },
